          ":question/id": "19a4e47b-fcd5-50aa-804a-b275eeea23d5",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-106691",
          ":question/problem": "A factory's management noted that the growth of the unit production is directly proportional to the difference between the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce and the current production quantity. Let $P(t)$ be the production quantity, measured in units produced, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in days. If the maximum number of units that a worker can produce is $70$ $\\text{units/day}$, which differential equation could be used to model the factory's production? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The rate at which the production $P(t)$ of the factory changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce and the current production quantity. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=70\\text{\u00a0units/day},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(70-P).\n$$"
