          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-344228",
          ":question/problem": "A laboratory incubator initially contains $18$ bacterial cultures. The number of cultures in the incubator $B(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in days. If the maximum capacity of the incubator is $50$ cultures, which differential equation could be used to model the number of cultures? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Since the number of cultures in the incubator obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of cultures changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of cultures in the incubator. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-B),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the incubator, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=50$ cultures, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(50-B).\n$$"
