          ":question/id": "56f2ee29-f876-5b21-80e6-83404415a207",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-344215",
          ":question/problem": "A hospital administrator observed that the growth of the number of occupied beds in a hospital is directly proportional to the difference between the hospital\u2019s maximum bed capacity and the current number of occupied beds. Let $H(t)$ be the number of occupied beds, measured in beds, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum number of beds the hospital can support is $75$ beds, which differential equation could be used to model the number of occupied beds in the hospital? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The rate at which the number of occupied beds $H(t)$ in the hospital changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of beds the hospital can support and the current number of occupied beds. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-H),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of beds the hospital can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=75\\text{\u00a0beds},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k(75-H).\n$$"
